{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310821,"nonce":"wgllIjeLULsSWLo1dex2T2uBgto=","title":"Cross-codebase study of Zyntax vs Rayzor. Zyntax's mandelbrot has NO objects so that comparison is void; its SRA is WEAKER than rayzor's ...","message":"- **Zyntax SRA** (`crates/compiler/src/scalar_replace_alloc.rs`) is v1\n  SINGLE-BLOCK by design; cross-block phi aborts, non-constant GEP aborts.\n  Rayzor's `scalar_replacement.rs` is function-wide AND has\n  `run_phi_sra_on_function` flattening a phi over several allocations. Copying\n  theirs is a downgrade.\n- **Zyntax drop insertion** (`drop_insert.rs`) only frees when the malloc and\n  every use sit in ONE block — \"we'd rather miss frees than insert wrong ones\".\n  Anything escaping its block is never freed. Part of rayzor's 31.6% `free` cost\n  is the price of being CORRECT where Zyntax leaks.\n- **Value-struct default** is not adoptable: a Haxe class instance is a\n  reference with observable identity (`==`, escaping stores, the type-id header\n  written in `mir/helpers/alloc.rs`). Would need a `@:struct`-style language\n  extension.\n\n\n**ATTEMPTED — the blind spot is REAL but the fix is not one line. Patch kept at\n`scratchpad/escape_heap_aware.rs`.** Extending the match to\n`CallDirect { func_id in malloc_ids }` works as an ANALYSIS: on mandelbrot the\npass went from seeing 0 allocations to seeing 3 per outer loop and 1 in the\ninner, and on a synthetic non-escaping loop alloc it reported\n\"1 allocs, 0 escape, 1 hoistable\" and fired.\n\nBut the HOIST is unsound as written: moving the `CallDirect` to the preheader\nleaves its operands behind, so the size constant is still defined in the loop\nbody and the LLVM tier dies with `IrId(9) not found in LLVM value_map`, silently\nfalling back to Cranelift. **Hoisting a call requires hoisting its operands\ntoo** — `Alloc` never needed this because its size is an immediate. Fix that\nbefore retrying.\n\nAnd it would NOT have helped mandelbrot anyway: all 4 allocations there\nlegitimately escape (the inner one is the rotation, which crosses the back edge,\nso rule 3 rejects it correctly). The lever needs a non-escaping loop-body\nallocation to pay off.\n\nOriginal finding: `compiler/src/ir/escape_analysis.rs:48` matches only `IrInstruction::Alloc`,\nwhich in rayzor is a STACK SLOT. Every Haxe `new` lowers to a `CallDirect` to\nextern malloc (`mir/helpers/alloc.rs`). **So LICM's alloc-hoisting path is dead\ncode for class instances.** Extending that match to also accept\n`CallDirect { func_id in malloc_ids }` — the same two-arm shape\n`scalar_replacement.rs` already uses — would let LICM hoist a non-escaping\nloop-body `new` into the preheader and reuse ONE buffer across all iterations\ninstead of malloc/free per iteration. Highest-value item, no language change.\n\n\n- **Zyntax's lowering emits NO zero-init** — one malloc, then one GEP+Store per\n  field, exactly the shape its SRA matcher accepts. Rayzor emits size/8 extra\n  Const+GEP(I64)+Store triples per `new`, and rayzor's OWN comment says that is\n  what \"kept Complex out of SRA\". Partly addressed by 9cfad8d0 (slot 0), but the\n  remaining field zeros are still emitted.\n- **Paired benchmark**: add `mandelbrot_value.hx` — mandelbrot.hx with Complex\n  hand-scalarized to four Floats and the palette as a function. The delta\n  against mandelbrot.hx measures the recoverable allocation cost directly, and\n  makes the Zyntax comparison honest. Model it on their\n  bench_nbody/bench_nbody_ref pair (a one-line diff), NOT their op_overload pair\n  whose loop bounds differ 10x.\n- **Array pool placement** (their `ssa.rs`): one contiguous buffer for an array\n  literal of N heap structs instead of N mallocs. No rayzor analogue; common\n  Haxe shape (`for (i in 0...n) arr.push(new C(...))`).\n\nRelated: [[perf_rayzor_vs_zyntax_nuc_baseline]],\n[[perf_mandelbrot_45x_tierup_regression]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_zyntax_study_findings` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}